28.29523031752478 167,227.90317540321212 168,227.4986486387774 169,227.0814856526544 170,226.65151389911682 171,226.20855223058857 172,225.75241043630743 173,225.28288874421332 174,224.7997772825746 175,224.30285549745878 176,223.79189152169246 177,223.26664149042944 178,222.72684879784524 179,222.17224328879004 180,221.60254037844385 181,221.01744009211154 182,220.4166260162505 183,219.79976415061543 184,219.16650165000323 185,218.51646544245034 186,217.8492607088319 187,217.16446920658586 188,216.4616474176652 189,215.7403244977378 190,215 191,214.24014134263012 192,213.46018098373213 193,212.6595132614157 194,211.83749084919418 195,210.9934207678533 196,210.1265598839718 197,209.23610981186988 198,208.32121111929345 199,207.3809367167903 200,206.4142842854285 201,205.42016756583303 202,204.3974062915899 203,203.34471449936711 204,202.26068688320095 205,201.14378277661478 206,199.9923072370877 207,198.8043885637971 208,197.57795138864805 209,196.31068422387733 210,195 211,193.642987645583 212,192.23635208501673 213,190.77633906953736 214,189.25863986500212 215,187.6782687642637 216,186.02940328869227 217,184.30517214248417 218,182.49736834815167 219,180.596052460712 220,178.58898943540675 221,176.46082488325578 222,174.19183588453086 223,171.75595189897822 224,169.11744421846396 225,166.224989991992 226,163 227,159.31049156228644 228,154.89974874213243 229,149.1067359796659 230,135\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#202832\" stroke-width=\"1\"></polyline><polyline points=\"130,135 180,48.39745962155614\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b33a36\" stroke-width=\"2\"></polyline><path d=\"M 163.33333333333334,135\n     A 33.333333333333336 33.333333333333336 0 0 0 146.66666666666669,106.13248654051871\" stroke=\"#202832\" fill=\"none\"></path><polygon points=\"146.66666666666669,106.13248654051871\n     153.66666666666669,107.13248654051871\n     149.66666666666669,113.13248654051871\" fill=\"#202832\"></polygon><foreignObject x=\"226\" y=\"73\" width=\"90\" height=\"22\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mrow><mi>t</mi><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mtext>arc&nbsp;length</mtext><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></mrow></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"159.33333333333334\" y=\"98.33333333333333\" width=\"70\" height=\"22\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mrow><mi>t</mi><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mtext>rad</mtext><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></mrow></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"234\" y=\"113\" width=\"20\" height=\"22\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>A</mi></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"180\" y=\"28.397459621556138\" width=\"150\" height=\"30\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace></mrow><mi>t</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace></mrow><mi>t</mi><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"10\" y=\"111\" width=\"35\" height=\"22\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow></msub></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"95\" y=\"11\" width=\"45\" height=\"22\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"135\" y=\"235\" width=\"55\" height=\"22\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></math></div></foreignObject></svg><figcaption>Definition of cosine and sine.</figcaption></figure>","hidden":true,"type":"code"},{"input":"!!! definition\n\tThe *radian measure* of ``\\angle AOP_t`` is ``t`` radians.\n\nIn calculus it is assumed that all angles are measured in radians unless degrees or other units are stated explicitly. When we talk about the angle ``\\frac{\\uppi }{3}`` we mean ``\\frac{\\uppi }{3}`` radians (which is ``60°``), not ``\\frac{\\uppi }{3}`` degrees.\n\n!!! example\n\t*Arc length* and *sector area*.\n\n\tAn arc of a circle of radius ``r`` subtends an angle ``t`` at the centre of the circle. Find the length ``s`` of the arc and the area ``A`` of the sector lying between the arc and the centre of the circle.\n\n\tThe length ``s`` of the arc is the same fraction of the circumference ``2\\uppi  r`` of the circle that the angle ``t`` is to a complete revolution ``2\\uppi `` radians. Thus,\n\t```math\n\ts=\\frac{t}{2\\uppi }(2\\uppi  r)=rt\\,.\n\t```\n\tSimilarly, the area ``A`` of the circular sector is the same fraction of the area ``\\uppi  r^2`` of the whole circle:\n\t```math\n\tA=\\frac{t}{2\\uppi }(\\uppi  r^2)=\\frac{r^2t}{2}\\,.\n\t```\n\nUsing the procedure described above, we can find the point ``P_t`` corresponding to any real number ``t``.\n\n!!! definition\n\tFor any real ``t``, the *cosine* of ``t`` denoted by ``\\cos t`` and the *sine* of ``t`` denoted by ``\\sin t`` are the ``x``- and the ``y``-coordinates of the point ``P_t``.\n\nBecause they are defined this way, cosine and sine are often called the *circular functions*. Note that these definitions agree with the ones given earlier for an acute angle.\n\nMany important properties of ``\\cos t`` and ``\\sin t`` follow from the fact that they are coordinates of the point ``P_t`` on the circle ``C`` with equation ``x^2+y^2=1``.\n\n!!! theorem\n\tLet ``t\\in\\mathbb{R}``.\n\t1. ``-1\\le\\cos t\\le 1`` and ``-1\\le\\sin t\\le 1``\n\t2. ``\\cos^2 t+\\sin^2 t=1`` (Pythagorean identity)\n\t3. ``\\cos(t+2\\uppi )=\\cos t`` and ``\\sin(t+2\\uppi )=\\sin t`` (periodicity)\n\t4. ``\\cos(-t)=\\cos(t)`` and ``\\sin(-t)=-\\sin t`` (cosine is an *even* function, sine is an *odd* function)\n\t5. ``\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\uppi }{2}-t\\right)=\\sin t`` and ``\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\uppi }{2}-t\\right)=\\cos t`` (*complementary angles*, symmetry about ``y=x``)\n\t6. ``\\cos(\\uppi -t)=-\\cos(t)`` and ``\\sin(\\uppi -t)=\\sin t`` (*supplementary angles*, symmetry about ``x=0``)\n\nThe next table summarizes the most important values of cosine and sine.\n\n|Radians|``0``|``\\frac{\\uppi }{6}``|``\\frac{\\uppi }{4}``|``\\frac{\\uppi }{3}``|``\\frac{\\uppi }{2}``|``\\frac{2\\uppi }{3}``|``\\frac{3\\uppi }{4}``|``\\frac{5\\uppi }{6}``|``\\uppi ``|\n|:------|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|\n|Cosine|``1``|``\\frac{\\sqrt 3}{2}``|``\\frac{\\sqrt 2}{2}``|``\\frac{1}{2}``|``0``|``-\\frac{1}{2}``|``-\\frac{\\sqrt 2}{2}``|``-\\frac{\\sqrt 3}{2}``|``-1``|\n|Sine|``0``|``\\frac{1}{2}``|``\\frac{\\sqrt 2}{2}``|``\\frac{\\sqrt 3}{2}``|``1``|``\\frac{\\sqrt 3}{2}``|``\\frac{\\sqrt 2}{2}``|``\\frac{1}{2}``|``0``|\n\nObserve that the graph of ``\\sin x`` is the graph of ``\\cos x`` shifted to the right a distance ``\\frac{\\uppi }{2}``.","output":"","result":"DefinitionThe radian measure of ∠AOPt is t radians.\nIn calculus it is assumed that all angles are measured in radians unless degrees or other units are stated explicitly. When we talk about the angle π3 we mean π3 radians (which is 60°), not π3 degrees.\nExampleArc length and sector area.\nAn arc of a circle of radius r subtends an angle t at the centre of the circle. Find the length s of the arc and the area A of the sector lying between the arc and the centre of the circle.\nThe length s of the arc is the same fraction of the circumference 2πr of the circle that the angle t is to a complete revolution 2π radians. Thus,\ns=t2π(2πr)=rt.\nSimilarly, the area A of the circular sector is the same fraction of the area πr2 of the whole circle:\nA=t2π(πr2)=r2t2.\nUsing the procedure described above, we can find the point Pt corresponding to any real number t.\nDefinitionFor any real t, the cosine of t denoted by cos⁡t and the sine of t denoted by sin⁡t are the x- and the y-coordinates of the point Pt.\nBecause they are defined this way, cosine and sine are often called the circular functions. Note that these definitions agree with the ones given earlier for an acute angle.\nMany important properties of cos⁡t and sin⁡t follow from the fact that they are coordinates of the point Pt on the circle C with equation x2+y2=1.\nTheoremLet t∈ℝ.\n\n−1≤cos⁡t≤1 and −1≤sin⁡t≤1\n\ncos2⁡t+sin2⁡t=1 (Pythagorean identity)\n\ncos⁡(t+2π)=cos⁡t and sin⁡(t+2π)=sin⁡t (periodicity)\n\ncos⁡(−t)=cos⁡(t) and sin⁡(−t)=−sin⁡t (cosine is an even function, sine is an odd function)\n\ncos⁡(π2−t)=sin⁡t and sin⁡(π2−t)=cos⁡t (complementary angles, symmetry about y=x)\n\ncos⁡(π−t)=−cos⁡(t) and sin⁡(π−t)=sin⁡t (supplementary angles, symmetry about x=0)\n\n\nThe next table summarizes the most important values of cosine and sine.\nRadians0π6π4π3π22π33π45π6πCosine13222120−12−22−32−1Sine012223213222120\nObserve that the graph of sin⁡x is the graph of cos⁡x shifted to the right a distance π2.","resultHTML":"<aside class=\"markdown-admonition markdown-admonition-definition\"><div class=\"markdown-admonition-title\">Definition</div><div class=\"markdown-admonition-body\"><p>The <em>radian measure</em> of <math><mrow><mi>∠</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>O</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></math> is <math><mi>t</mi></math> radians.</p></div></aside>\n<p>In calculus it is assumed that all angles are measured in radians unless degrees or other units are stated explicitly. When we talk about the angle <math><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math> we mean <math><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math> radians (which is <math><mrow><mn>60</mn><mi>°</mi></mrow></math>), not <math><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math> degrees.</p>\n<aside class=\"markdown-admonition markdown-admonition-example\"><div class=\"markdown-admonition-title\">Example</div><div class=\"markdown-admonition-body\"><p><em>Arc length</em> and <em>sector area</em>.</p>\n<p>An arc of a circle of radius <math><mi>r</mi></math> subtends an angle <math><mi>t</mi></math> at the centre of the circle. Find the length <math><mi>s</mi></math> of the arc and the area <math><mi>A</mi></math> of the sector lying between the arc and the centre of the circle.</p>\n<p>The length <math><mi>s</mi></math> of the arc is the same fraction of the circumference <math><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math> of the circle that the angle <math><mi>t</mi></math> is to a complete revolution <math><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow></mrow></math> radians. Thus,</p>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>r</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>.</mi></mrow></math>\n<p>Similarly, the area <math><mi>A</mi></math> of the circular sector is the same fraction of the area <math><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mn class=\"tml-sml-pad\">2</mn></msup></mrow></math> of the whole circle:</p>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mn class=\"tml-sml-pad\">2</mn></msup><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mn class=\"tml-sml-pad\">2</mn></msup><mi>t</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>.</mi></mrow></math></div></aside>\n<p>Using the procedure described above, we can find the point <math><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></math> corresponding to any real number <math><mi>t</mi></math>.</p>\n<aside class=\"markdown-admonition markdown-admonition-definition\"><div class=\"markdown-admonition-title\">Definition</div><div class=\"markdown-admonition-body\"><p>For any real <math><mi>t</mi></math>, the <em>cosine</em> of <math><mi>t</mi></math> denoted by <math><mrow><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace></mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> and the <em>sine</em> of <math><mi>t</mi></math> denoted by <math><mrow><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace></mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> are the <math><mi>x</mi></math>- and the <math><mi>y</mi></math>-coordinates of the point <math><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></math>.</p></div></aside>\n<p>Because they are defined this way, cosine and sine are often called the <em>circular functions</em>. Note that these definitions agree with the ones given earlier for an acute angle.</p>\n<p>Many important properties of <math><mrow><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace></mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> and <math><mrow><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace></mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> follow from the fact that they are coordinates of the point <math><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></math> on the circle <math><mi>C</mi></math> with equation <math><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn class=\"tml-sml-pad\">2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn class=\"tml-sml-pad\">2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<aside class=\"markdown-admonition markdown-admonition-theorem\"><div class=\"markdown-admonition-title\">Theorem</div><div class=\"markdown-admonition-body\"><p>Let <math><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><math><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace></mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace></mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>\n</li>\n<li><math><mrow><msup><mi>cos</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>sin</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> (Pythagorean identity)\n</li>\n<li><math><mrow><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace></mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> and <math><mrow><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace></mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> (periodicity)\n</li>\n<li><math><mrow><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mi>t</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> and <math><mrow><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mi>t</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace></mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> (cosine is an <em>even</em> function, sine is an <em>odd</em> function)\n</li>\n<li><math><mrow><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace></mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> and <math><mrow><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace></mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> (<em>complementary angles</em>, symmetry about <math><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>)\n</li>\n<li><math><mrow><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> and <math><mrow><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace></mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> (<em>supplementary angles</em>, symmetry about <math><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>)\n</li>\n</ol></div></aside>\n<p>The next table summarizes the most important values of cosine and sine.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Radians</th><th><math><mn>0</mn></math></th><th><math><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mn>6</mn></mfrac></math></th><th><math><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math></th><th><math><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math></th><th><math><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math></th><th><math><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math></th><th><math><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math></th><th><math><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow></mrow><mn>6</mn></mfrac></math></th><th><math><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow></math></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cosine</td><td><math><mn>1</mn></math></td><td><math><mfrac><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math></td><td><math><mfrac><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math></td><td><math><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math></td><td><math><mn>0</mn></math></td><td><math><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></math></td><td><math><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mfrac><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></math></td><td><math><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mfrac><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></math></td><td><math><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Sine</td><td><math><mn>0</mn></math></td><td><math><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math></td><td><math><mfrac><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math></td><td><math><mfrac><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math></td><td><math><mn>1</mn></math></td><td><math><mfrac><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math></td><td><math><mfrac><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math></td><td><math><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math></td><td><math><mn>0</mn></math></td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>Observe that the graph of <math><mrow><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace></mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> is the graph of <math><mrow><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace></mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> shifted to the right a distance <math><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>.</p>","hidden":true,"type":"markdown"},{"input":"const xmid = 300;\nconst ymid = 80;\nconst scale = 50;\n\nconst figure = JSCells.figure(\n  { width: 625, height: 155 },\n  \"The graph of the cosine function in red and the sine function in blue.\"\n);\n\nfigure.axes({\n  origin: [xmid, ymid],\n  scale,\n  x: { ticks: [] },\n  y: { ticks: [-1, 1] },\n});\n\nfigure.plot((x) => Math.cos(x), {\n  domain: [-6, 7],\n  samples: 1301,\n  stroke: \"#b33a36\",\n  width: 2,\n});\n\nfigure.plot((x) => Math.sin(x), {\n  domain: [-6, 7],\n  samples: 1301,\n  stroke: \"#4169e1\",\n  width: 2,\n});\n\nconst xTicks = [\n  [String.raw`-\\frac{3\\uppi}{2}`, -3 * Math.PI / 2, 32],\n  [String.raw`-\\uppi`, -Math.PI, 32],\n  [String.raw`-\\frac{\\uppi}{2}`, -Math.PI / 2, 32],\n  [String.raw`\\frac{\\uppi}{2}`, Math.PI / 2, 18],\n  [String.raw`\\uppi`, Math.PI, 12],\n  [String.raw`\\frac{3\\uppi}{2}`, 3 * Math.PI / 2, 18],\n  [String.raw`2\\uppi`, 2 * Math.PI, 24],\n];\n\nfor (const [label, value, labelWidth] of xTicks) {\n  const x = xmid + scale * value;\n\n  // Tick mark on the x-axis.\n  figure\n    .line()\n    .x1(x)\n    .y1(ymid - 4)\n    .x2(x)\n    .y2(ymid + 4)\n    .stroke(\"#000000\")\n    .strokeWidth(1);\n\n  figure.label(label, {\n    x: x - labelWidth / 2,\n    y: ymid + 10,\n    latex: true,\n    width: labelWidth,\n    height: 32,\n  });\n}\n\nfigure;","output":"","result":"xy−11−3π2−π−π2π2π3π22πThe graph of the cosine function in red and the sine function in blue.","resultHTML":"<figure><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"625\" height=\"155\" viewBox=\"0 0 625 155\"><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"625\" y2=\"80\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><line x1=\"300\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"155\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><polygon points=\"625,80 618,76 618,84\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\"></polygon><polygon points=\"300,0 296,7 304,7\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\"></polygon><foreignObject x=\"611\" y=\"50\" width=\"14\" height=\"28\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>x</mi></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"308\" y=\"-2\" width=\"14\" height=\"28\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>y</mi></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"296\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"304\" y2=\"130\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"276\" y=\"118\" width=\"32\" height=\"32\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></